      "value": "@{6222c8bd759f2e2d1462eca6|full_name}: We've reproduced the issue in that world, and have some optimizations we can ship eventually. However, there will still be a noticable performance overhead. Some context:\n\nThis is happening because of a feature that prioritizes network events from visible objects. As such, syncing components such as Udon Behaviours with `SyncMode != None` or `VRCObjectSync` components will check all child renderers for if they are visible. This itself is something we cannot remove, even if we can optimize it a bit more. Frankly, having tens of thousands of Renderer components underneath a synced object is simply not a supported scenario.\n\nAs a suggestion, some alternatives that may work in your case and would not experience this issue, or limit it:\n\n- Don't have these child objects exist in the scene at all time, use Instantiate to spawn them in on-demand instead. Either from a base prefab, or I suppose you could have 60k prefabs and reference them, but that might run into its own kind of trouble.\n\n- Have the large hierarchy live outside of any synced objects. I.e. put a an empty GameObject directly in the scene root, have the 60k objects underneath, and reference them not from a parent Udon Behaviour but from a sibling.\n\n- Split the hierarchy into multiple groups of, say, 60x1000 objects, and disable the root GameObject with the syncing behaviour when none of the children are active. Disabled UdonBehaviours will not scan the hierarchy for Renderers. Might run into sync issues with a disabled UdonBehaviour though, we recommend keeping syncing behaviours active at all times. To mitigate that part, you could sync a sibling object and send data into the group root behaviours via events or similar.\n\nHope that helps!",
      "viewerReactions": null,
      "voteID": null
    },
